<SnowResource
  title="Commercial Snow Removal Trigger Depth Guide for Minnesota Properties"
  description="Understand commercial snow-removal trigger depths, event definitions, open-by expectations and how to write clearer Minnesota snow contracts before winter."
  kicker="SNOW TRIGGER DEPTH & EVENT TERMS"
>
  <p>A snow-removal trigger is the condition written into the commercial agreement that tells the contractor when the contracted snow scope applies. It is one of the most important parts of a snow contract because vague language creates different expectations for the property manager and service provider.</p>
  <p>A trigger should not be confused with a blanket response-time promise. Trigger depth, route sequence, operating priorities and completion expectations all need to be discussed in the context of the actual property.</p>

  <h2>Why “plow as needed” is too vague</h2>
  <p>Commercial sites have different tolerances for accumulation. A lightly used office property may not have the same operating needs as a medical building, retail center, warehouse with early shifts or campus with multiple entrances. A useful agreement states the trigger or event condition instead of leaving it to interpretation during a storm.</p>

  <h2>Common trigger approaches</h2>
  <h3>Zero-tolerance or trace-accumulation requests</h3>
  <p>Some properties ask to address accumulation or ice before a traditional plowing depth is reached. This can materially change service frequency, staffing and ice-management requirements. It should be explicitly priced and accepted rather than assumed.</p>

  <h3>One-inch trigger</h3>
  <p>A one-inch trigger can create more frequent service than a two-inch trigger and may be appropriate for properties with tighter operating requirements. The contractor still needs to understand sidewalks, de-icing, open-by priorities and what happens during continuing snowfall.</p>

  <h3>Two-inch trigger</h3>
  <p>A two-inch trigger is common in commercial discussions, but the number alone is not a complete scope. The agreement still needs to define which surfaces are included, how ice work is handled and whether additional visits occur during longer events.</p>

  <h3>Custom or event-based triggers</h3>
  <p>Some properties define service around event conditions, mixed precipitation, operating hours or other site requirements. If the property has a custom specification, provide it in the RFP so every bidder prices the same expectation.</p>

  <h2>Define the event—not just the depth</h2>
  <p>Per-event pricing is difficult to compare if each bidder uses a different definition of an event. The contract should explain how continuing snowfall, multiple waves of a storm, cleanup after snow stops and repeated ice conditions are treated.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>When does an event begin for billing and service purposes?</li>
    <li>When is an event considered complete?</li>
    <li>How are long-duration storms handled?</li>
    <li>Are repeat visits included or separately priced?</li>
    <li>Is post-storm cleanup part of the event?</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Add operating hours and priority access</h2>
  <p>The trigger tells the contractor when the contracted scope applies; it does not by itself identify which part of the property matters most. During the site walk, identify priority entrances, employee access, loading areas, fire exits and any requested open-by time so the proposal can address actual operations.</p>
  <div class="callout"><strong>Website requests are not guarantees.</strong> Entering a preferred trigger or open-by time on JSM’s form gives the team information for proposal planning. Route capacity, property location, scope and the final agreement still have to be reviewed and accepted.</div>

  <h2>Sidewalk and ice triggers may be different</h2>
  <p>Pedestrian surfaces can require treatment under conditions that do not match a parking-lot plowing trigger. Refreeze, freezing rain and tracked snow can create separate ice-management needs. Commercial agreements should make those conditions visible rather than assuming the lot trigger controls every surface.</p>

  <h2>Put the trigger on the property map or scope</h2>
  <p>For complex sites, pair the written trigger with a marked site plan showing lots, walks, entrances, loading areas and snow-storage zones. That makes it easier for the property manager, contractor and on-site contacts to work from one winter reference.</p>

  <h2>Questions to settle before signing</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>What trigger depth or event condition applies?</li>
    <li>Does the same trigger apply to sidewalks and ice treatment?</li>
    <li>What surfaces are included?</li>
    <li>What happens during extended snowfall?</li>
    <li>How are additional de-icing applications authorized?</li>
    <li>What snow-storage, relocation or hauling work is outside the base scope?</li>
  </ul>
